KW  - Physical-informed neural networks
KW  - Neural tangent kernel
KW  - Feature encoding
KW  - Wave propagation
KW  - Arbitrary source location
KW  - Delayed excitation
AB  - Physical-informed neural network (PINN) has become an efficient means for forward modelling seismic wave propagation in recent years. For the multi-source models with arbitrary position and excitation times, conventional PINNs lack the ability of generalized learning. Inspired by supervised learning, a novel architecture of PINN comprising a two-part network is proposed in this study, which is called the encoded multi-source (EMS) PINN. The Deep neural network 1 (DNN1) of EMS PINN is designed to rapidly predict two initial snapshots with an arbitrary position and delay time at specific moments. These two snapshots can be used for constraining the initial condition (IC) of the multi-source model. Specifically, feature encoding is applied in DNN1 to efficiently and generally learn the characteristics of the multi-source model via transformed input variables in the spatial domain. The Deep neural network 2 (DNN2) of EMS PINN has considered the physical information which can be used for predicting the wavefield of propagation in the whole spatiotemporal domain. Meanwhile, the neural tangent kernel theory (NTK) is adopted to self-adaptively adjust the weights of each loss term during the training process. In addition, the generalized learning performance of the conventional PINN and the EMS PINN in solving the multi-source wave problem was compared from the NTK perspective. The comparison results show that the EMS PINN has superior performance for efficiently and accurately modelling multi-source wave propagation.
ER  - 

