UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0952197625031744
KW  - Single sample face recognition
KW  - Convolutional network
KW  - Gabor filters
KW  - Orthogonality
KW  - Feature extraction
AB  - Designing predefined filters based convolutional neural network (PFCNN) is an important branch of lightweight network, like principal component analysis network. However, their feature dimensionality rises sharply when the network stage deepens. Moreover, although the scattering structure can extract multi-scale information implicitly, but it will lead to redundant and non-informative features. To alleviate the drawback, we proposed a novel one-stage PFCNN method with orthogonal Gabor filters dubbed OrthGabor for illumination robust single sample face recognition, which has three advantages. Firstly, performing SVD-based orthogonalization on the Gabor filters not only introduces the importance-ordering among the filters, which addresses the limitations of uniform significance in conventional Gabor filters, but also enhances the discriminative power of the learned features. Secondly, our method enables the introduction of multi-scale information within a single filter size, eliminating the need for scattering structure design. Thirdly, the single-stage convolution operation makes our method computationally simple and efficient, while also resulting in lower feature dimensionality. We have obtained three key findings. (1) Simply orthogonalizing the Gabor filters can significantly enhance recognition performance. (2) Unlike conventional PFCNN, which implicitly generates multi-scale information through scattering, explicitly incorporating multi-scale information may be more reasonable, as it does not produce redundant or ineffective filters. (3) Zero-phase component analysis (ZCA) preprocessing plays a critical role in Gabor-based PFCNN methods. Extensive experiments were conducted on three illumination face datasets, the results demonstrate that our OrthGabor achieves higher recognition performance than Gabor, related PFCNNs, and many state-of-the-art methods, indicating that OrthGabor has stronger robustness against illumination variation.
