UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0952197625031665
KW  - Complex networks
KW  - Neural networks
KW  - Representation learning
KW  - Data mining
AB  - Drug repositioning offers a cost-effective strategy in the realm of comprehensive drug development, and efficient computational methods could provide novel therapeutic indication discovery of approved drugs. However, there exist two major challenges in drug repositioning as follows: shallow representation methods fail to exploit rich semantic and high-order topological information from heterogeneous biomedical graphs. Current methods focus narrowly on topological or semantic views of drug–disease interactions, while overlooking multi-scale structural patterns that reflect diverse connectivity and functional pathways. To address the issues, we proposed a deep semantic and structural feature-aware drug repositioning method with heterogeneous frequency-domain contrastive regularization learning to deal with the rich connectivity patterns of nodes, which jointly capture local dependencies, global semantics, and multi-scale structural patterns based on heterogeneous biological information networks. First, a dynamic gated graph attention mechanism is devised to regulate the information enhancement of neighbor nodes, where it could mitigate over-smoothing and enhance node discriminability. Then, a semantic-structural feature extraction module is designed to extract attribute-level and long-range dependencies. In addition, we introduced a novel loss function that integrates frequency-domain contrastive regularization to capture discriminative representations across multiple scales, and the regularization module decomposes graph signals into different frequency components, facilitates contrastive learning in the frequency domain, and leverages interaction patterns in negative samples to learn more discriminative representations. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method outperforms competitive baselines in drug repositioning and could offer valuable insights for biomedical knowledge discovery. Comprehensive case studies further validate the effectiveness of the proposed model in discovering potential drug–disease associations.
