DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.engappai.2025.113140
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0952197625031719
KW  - Dynamic spatio-temporal graph
KW  - Remaining useful life
KW  - Graph neural network
AB  - In the health monitoring of rotating mechanical equipment, single-channel signal monitoring is more economical in practical production settings. Current remaining useful life prediction methods sometimes encounter difficulties in thoroughly extracting temporal dynamic characteristics and latent structural information from single-channel signals, resulting in an inadequate characterization of their dynamic deterioration processes. To address the challenge of predicting bearing remaining life under single-source sensor scenarios, this paper proposes a degradation-aware multi-scale dynamic graph representation learning framework named Multi-scale Dynamic Feature Graph and Multi-scale Topology Temporal Enhancement Network (M2TEN). Unlike existing methods relying on fixed topologies or multi-channel inputs, M2TEN reconstructs degraded topologies and adaptively updates edge weights under single-channel conditions, enhancing interpretable modeling of complex degradation mechanisms. This framework comprises two major modules: Multi-Scale Dynamic Feature Graph Construction (MS-DFG) and Multi-Scale Topological Time-Series Enhancement (MTTEN). The MS-DFG module constructs a multi-scale dynamic sparse graph using statistical features from multiple time windows as nodes, combined with cosine similarity thresholds and pearson correlation. This captures the temporal evolution characteristics and deep structural features embedded in the signal. The MTTEN module organically integrates graph attention networks, graph autoencoders, and bidirectional long short-term memory techniques to uniformly model node criticality, topological structure, and temporal evolution while maintaining scale consistency, enabling collaborative representation from local mutations to global trends. Experimental results demonstrate that the M2TEN framework substantially enhances the accuracy and robustness of bearing life degradation predictions in single-source sensor situations, offering a novel solution to the challenge of single-source signal characterization in equipment health management.
ER  - 

