KW  - Semi-supervised learning
KW  - Remote sensing object detection
KW  - Deep learning
KW  - Pseudo-label
KW  - Teacher–student network
AB  - Semi-supervised learning has been successfully processed a large amount of remote sensing data and reduced the cost of manual annotation. However, significant differences in classes and severe class imbalance in remote sensing datasets limit the effectiveness of object detection based on semi-supervised learning. To tackle these challenges, this paper introduces a semi-supervised learning framework for large-scale imbalanced remote sensing object detection. We construct a teacher–student network that uses a triple exponential moving average for timely updates, improving feature extraction for remote sensing objects. Then, a two-stage class adaptation module is designed. It includes candidate generation and threshold setting to filter suitable pseudo-labels for all classes, especially the tail classes. This approach addresses the low recall issue stemming from class imbalance. Finally, we propose a novel semi-supervised loss function to speed up model convergence. Experiments show our method achieves mean average precision at intersection over union 0.5 (mAP50) of 75.8% and mean average precision at intersection over union from 0.5 to 0.95 (mAP50-95) of 51.5% on remote sensing dataset. The visual results are free of detection errors, confirming its precision in detecting objects in highly imbalanced remote sensing data. This semi-supervised learning framework can reduce annotation costs and improve detection accuracy, which could be applied to object detection tasks on large-scale remote sensing data.
